<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tekle Haymanot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1215–1313</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Takla Haymanot</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tekle Haymanot was an Ethiopian monk and saint, one of the most revered figures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church">Ethiopian Orthodox Church</a>. Saints' lives describe him preaching in the pagan kingdom of Damot and founding the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-libanos">Debre Libanos</a> in Shewa, which became the leading monastic house of the Ethiopian church. He was later credited with helping to restore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a> in 1270.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tekle-haymanot-saint.jpg" alt="Tekle Haymanot" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abune_Tekele_Haymanot.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
